NAME
    passwd - change a password (root only)

SYNOPSIS
    passwd [user]

DESCRIPTION
    Changes a password. With no argument, changes the current user's; with a
    name, that account's. ROOT ONLY: it rewrites /etc/shadow, which is mode
    0600 and root-owned, so a non-root caller could not write it even if the
    tool let them try.

    A user changing their OWN password therefore needs a privileged path -
    something that will do the write on their behalf after checking who is
    asking. That is a separate piece of work (an account server); until it
    lands, passwords are set by root.

    Prompts for the new password twice (echo off), derives a fresh salt, and
    stores SHA-256(salt || password) in /etc/shadow. The /etc/passwd line is
    not touched at all, so a password change cannot disturb uid, gid or home.

    The salt comes from the machine's hardware RNG where there is one; without
    it the tool falls back to a weaker clock-derived salt and says so.

    THAT FILE IS ONLY PROTECTED ON ext2. FAT32 and exFAT record no mode at all,
    so the filesystem server has nothing to enforce: on those volumes any user
    can read the hashes AND overwrite root's entry. This is not new -
    /etc/passwd was equally writable there before the secrets moved out - and
    it cannot be fixed on a filesystem with no permission model. login says so
    at the prompt when it detects one.

EXAMPLES
    # passwd user
    New password: ****
    Retype new password: ****
    passwd: password updated for user

SEE ALSO
    useradd, usermod, groupadd, id, su, login
